Subject: [PATCH 3/6] fix(ui): don't treat an unreadable power state as
 "asleep"

Base stations whose power characteristic doesn't advertise the read
property (the norm on Windows, where notifications aren't supported
either) never report a power state, so power_state stays -1 forever.
The UI treated -1 as "not awake", which caused two problems:

- The status dot showed red/asleep for a station that is very likely
  powered on - the app was stating something it has no way to know.
- Every power toggle sent "awake", because "not awake" implied
  "asleep". Clicking the button on a station that was already on did
  nothing visible, so the station could never be turned off.

Treat -1 as its own "unknown" state: show it with a neutral dot, and
have toggles assume such a station may be on, so they send "sleep"
first. Once we've written a state ourselves we know it, and the
toggle behaves normally from then on.

Shared between the per-station, group and "power all" toggles via
frontend/src/lib/powerState.ts so the three can't drift apart.

(cherry picked from commit 16602e1052212403f59346de3f855a448323bfa7)
